## ch-atom | Atoms and the periodic table | chem | 0 | - | -
story 5 | Mendeleev left gaps for elements nobody had found
- 1869: Dmitri Mendeleev arranged elements by atomic weight and chemical behaviour, leaving blanks.
- He predicted the properties of the missing ones, including "eka-aluminium".
- *Gallium was discovered in 1875 with almost exactly the properties he'd predicted.*
> The table's shape comes from quantum mechanics

explain 3 | Why the periodic table has its shape
- Rows fill electron shells; the blocks (s, p, d, f) correspond to the type of orbital being filled.
- Elements in a column have the same outer electron arrangement, so they react alike.
- *That's why sodium and potassium both explode in water.*
> Noble gases barely react

quiz 2 | Which group of elements rarely forms compounds because their outer shells are full?
* Noble gases
o Alkali metals
o Halogens
o Transition metals
= Helium, neon, argon and co. Xenon does form a few compounds; the first was made in 1962.
> Most of an atom is empty space

explain 3 | If an atom were a stadium, the nucleus would be a pea
- The nucleus is about 100,000 times smaller across than the atom.
- But it holds over 99.9% of the mass.
- *Squeeze out the empty space from every human and all of humanity would fit in a sugar cube* (roughly, as neutron-star-like matter).
> Isotopes: same element, different neutrons

explain 2 | Isotopes
- Same number of protons (so same element), different numbers of neutrons.
- Carbon-12 and carbon-14 behave identically in chemistry; C-14 is radioactive.
- *Heavy water (with deuterium) is about 11% denser than normal water: heavy-water ice sinks.*
> Next: chemical bonds

## ch-bond | Chemical bonds | chem | 0 | - | ch-atom
explain 3 | Three kinds of bond
- Ionic: electrons transferred (Na⁺Cl⁻, table salt).
- Covalent: electrons shared (H₂O, CH₄).
- Metallic: a sea of shared electrons, which is why metals conduct and bend.
> Water's shape is everything

explain 4 | Why water is weird
- Its bent shape and polar O–H bonds make water a strong dipole that forms hydrogen bonds.
- That gives it a high boiling point for its size, high surface tension, and ice that floats.
- *If ice sank, lakes would freeze from the bottom up.*
> Carbon forms the most compounds

quiz 3 | Diamond and graphite are both pure carbon. Why is graphite soft?
* Its carbon atoms form flat sheets held together only weakly
o It contains impurities
o Its atoms are larger
o It's a liquid crystal
= Within a sheet, bonds are strong. Between sheets, weak forces let them slide.
= A single sheet is graphene.
> Next: reactions

## ch-react | Reactions and energy | chem | 0 | - | ch-bond
explain 3 | Activation energy: why paper doesn't burst into flames
- Burning paper releases energy overall, but needs a push to get started.
- Catalysts lower that energy barrier without being used up.
- *Enzymes are biological catalysts that can speed reactions by many orders of magnitude.*
> The Haber process feeds half the world

story 5 | The Haber–Bosch process feeds billions
- Making ammonia from nitrogen in the air (around 1910) enabled synthetic fertiliser on a massive scale.
- Roughly half the nitrogen in your body passed through a Haber–Bosch plant.
- *Fritz Haber also led Germany's WWI chemical weapons programme.* A complicated legacy.
> Le Chatelier's principle

explain 3 | Le Chatelier's principle
- Push a system at equilibrium and it shifts to partly undo the push.
- Add more reactant: more product forms. Heat an exothermic reaction: it shifts backward.
- *Fizzy drinks go flat when opened: lower pressure pushes CO₂ out of solution.*
> pH is a log scale

quiz 3 | Lemon juice has pH 2, coffee about pH 5. How much more acidic is lemon juice?
* About 1,000 times
o About 3 times
o About 2.5 times
o About 30 times
= pH is −log₁₀[H⁺]. Three pH units = 10³.
> Next: chemistry of everyday life

## ch-life | Kitchen chemistry | chem | 0 | - | ch-react
explain 4 | The Maillard reaction makes browned food taste good
- Amino acids and sugars react above about 140°C, creating hundreds of flavour compounds.
- Toast, seared steak, roasted coffee, beer's colour.
- *Boiled food doesn't brown because water can't get much above 100°C.*
> Why onions make you cry

explain 3 | Onion tears
- Cutting an onion breaks cells, letting an enzyme make syn-propanethial-S-oxide, a volatile irritant.
- It reaches your eyes and triggers tears.
- *Chilling the onion slows the reaction; a sharp knife breaks fewer cells.*
> Soap works by having two ends

explain 3 | How soap works
- Soap molecules have a water-loving head and an oil-loving tail.
- Tails bury into grease; heads face the water, forming little balls (micelles) that rinse away.
- *That's why soap tears apart viruses with fatty outer envelopes, like coronaviruses.*
> Salt melts ice

quiz 3 | Why does salt melt ice on roads?
* It lowers water's freezing point
o It reacts with ice to release heat
o It absorbs sunlight
o It raises the temperature of the road
= Dissolved salt disrupts ice formation. Below about −21°C, salty water freezes anyway.
> Next: the chemistry of life and drugs

## ch-org | Organic and medicinal chemistry | chem | 0 | - | ch-life
story 4 | Aspirin came from willow bark
- Willow bark was used for pain and fever for thousands of years; its active ingredient is related to salicylic acid.
- Bayer chemists made acetylsalicylic acid, sold as Aspirin from 1899.
- *Its mechanism (blocking COX enzymes) was only worked out in 1971.*
> Chirality: left-handed and right-handed molecules

explain 5 | Mirror-image molecules can act very differently
- Many molecules come in left- and right-handed versions, like gloves.
- Carvone: one form smells of spearmint, its mirror image of caraway.
- *Thalidomide's tragedy in the late 1950s led to much stricter testing of drugs.*
> Kekulé's dream

story 4 | Benzene's ring came in a dream (allegedly)
- August Kekulé said the ring structure of benzene came to him in a daydream of a snake biting its own tail.
- He told the story at a celebration in 1890, 25 years after proposing the structure.
- *Some historians doubt the story, but it's chemistry's most famous dream.*
> Chemistry track complete
